[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1729","stub":{"id":"1729","section":"1. Indian Boundary to Below Pigs in Space","altname":"Upper Citico","river":"Citico Creek","difficulty":"IIItoIV","states":["USA-TNN"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"1.12","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"108314","location":{"latitude":"35.4043","longitude":"-84.0788"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"113555","location":{"latitude":"35.4457","longitude":"-84.1094"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"4","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"101253956","title":null,"post_date":"2018-12-02 05:00:00+00","detail":"The right side boof (at the rapid described with the far side boof as best option at lower levels) has a log hindering the boof just below the surface at the 2.2 level we ran on 12/1/18. I hit it while charging the boof and it caused me to pitch over on my left side downstream while my stern broached behind me. I was stable but stuck in a sideways pin at the top of the drop with most of the current running under me. I had my hand on the log under me which helped me remain stable until Jim and Rob assisted so I could crawl out. Rob had piled up on me broached as well until Jim pulled him clear from the shore. The current took my freed left leg into a hyper-extension while I pulled my right leg out and dropped into the pool. There is more wood beneath the surface in the pool. Use caution while this wood is there. Better to take the middle line. I was lucky to get out with a sprained knee joint.","user":{"name":"Justin Kenagy"}},{"id":"101251450","title":null,"post_date":"2014-12-24 05:00:00+00","detail":"As of 12/15/14 there are 4 mandatory portages due to river wide wood. Use caution and scout before putting on.","user":{"name":"William Thomas"}}],"photos":[{"id":"6897","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"1787","description":"Looking back upstream at the final ledge in this rapid.","caption":"Pigs in Space","poi_name":"Pigs in Space","subject":"Helge Klockow","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6897","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6897"}},{"id":"8819","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5690","description":"Jim Strickland runs one of the many unnamed, fluffy, friendly rapids on Citico.","caption":"(RM) Unnamed Rapid","poi_name":"","subject":"Jim Strickland","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8819","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8819"}},{"id":"6894","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"1786","description":"The low water bridge at the put-in can be used to \r\ngauge flows:\r\n* If the water is lapping over the bridge, it is a low \r\ndoable level. \r\n* 10-12 inches over the bridge is ideal for a fast \r\nfun ride(roughly 1500 cfs on the tellico gauge, if \r\nthe rain fall is spread evenly throughout that \r\nregion) \r\n* 24 inches over the bride is fast, hole dodging \r\naction","caption":"put-in bridge gauge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Tom O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6894","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6894"}},{"id":"8818","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5690","description":"Jim Strickland runs the bottom drop of Boulder Dash as Duncan Cottrell scouts from shore. Boulder Dash gets its name from a large cottage size boulder on river left at the top of the rapid. The boulder makes the rapid seem more intimidating than it is since you can't see around it.","caption":"(RM) Boulder Dash","poi_name":"Boulder Dash","subject":"Jim Strickland and Jim Leutenegger","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8818","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8818"}},{"id":"11773","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6792","description":"This is the runout portion of Gorilla Face, the first major rapid (but not the first rapid) below the put-in. This rapid is recognized by the large boulder that sits to the left at the beginning. A distinctive gorilla profile can be seen in the boulder from the roadside.  ","caption":"Gorilla Face (Citico Creek, TN)","poi_name":"Gorilla Face","subject":"Hank Zachary","author":"Ken Strickland","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/11773","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/11773"}},{"id":"8817","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5690","description":"Jim Leutenegger running the first rapid after the put-in bridge.","caption":"(RM) 1st Rapid","poi_name":"1st Rapid","subject":"Jim Leutenegger","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8817","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8817"}},{"id":"8820","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5690","description":"Citico is one of the more picturesque runs in the southeast.","caption":"(RM) A view from the river","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8820","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8820"}},{"id":"883033","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"885728","description":"The namesake boulder for Gorilla Face. The line is to the right of the boulder.","caption":"Gorilla Face","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Ken Str